comments.body:
Thanks for the report! Unfortunately this is what happens when tasks have aliases and DisableRegistrationAliasing is disabled. The docs recommend you turn the flag on, but it's easy to miss and the docs don't warn about the problem you're running into. 

We could turn DisableRegistrationAliasing on by default, but it would be a breaking change - I'll talk to the team. The least we could do is improve the docs and emit a warning when these collisions happen.

@amit-writes-code welcome and thanks for the PR! I don't think it addresses the underlying issue, which is that "aliases + DisableRegistrationAliasing disabled = sad times". But I'll double check.
